==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*friþuz}}, ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*prāy-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*prēy-||like, love}}. Cognates include Old Frisian ''fretho'', Old Saxon ''[[frithu]]'' (Dutch ''[[vrede]]''), Old High German ''[[fridu]]'' (German ''[[Frieden]])'', Old Norse ''[[friðr]]'' (Icelandic ''[[friður]]'', Swedish and Danish ''[[fred]]''). The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek [[πραυς]], Common Slavic ''*prijati'' (Old Church Slavonic {{term|приꙗти|lang=cu}}, *{{term||prijatel}} ‘friend’, Old Irish ''ríar'' (Welsh [[rhydd]] ‘free’) and Albanian [[praj]], [[Prenda]].

